  "global_context_pack": "Core Thesis: An explainable cross-plant transfer learning framework predicts transmembrane pressure (TMP) in data-limited pilot-scale membrane bioreactors (MBRs) by pretraining LSTM and XGBoost models on three data-rich source plants to capture shared extracellular polymeric substance (EPS) fouling patterns and adaptively recalibrating plant-specific mechanisms with limited target-plant fine-tuning data.\n\nOutput Language: English\n\nGlossary of Acronyms and Terms:\n- MBR: Membrane Bioreactor\n- TMP: Transmembrane Pressure\n- EPS: Extracellular Polymeric Substances\n- EPSc: Extracellular Polymeric Substance Carbohydrates / Polysaccharides\n- EPSp: Extracellular Polymeric Substance Proteins\n- SMP: Soluble Microbial Products\n- SMPc: Soluble Microbial Product Carbohydrates\n- SMPp: Soluble Microbial Product Proteins\n- MLSS: Mixed Liquor Suspended Solids\n- MLVSS: Mixed Liquor Volatile Suspended Solids\n- HRT: Hydraulic Retention Time\n- SRT: Solids Retention Time\n- FLUX: Membrane Permeate Flux\n- TCOD / SCOD: Total / Soluble Chemical Oxygen Demand\n- LSTM: Long Short-Term Memory\n- XGBoost: Extreme Gradient Boosting\n- FT: Fine-Tuning\n- SHAP: SHapley Additive exPlanations\n- LOFO: Leave-One-Feature-Out\n- PCA: Principal Component Analysis\n- CIP: Clean-in-Place\n- LC-OCD: Liquid Chromatography–Organic Carbon Detection\n- EEM: Excitation-Emission Matrix\n\nDocument Outline & Structure:\n- Abstract: Motivation of fouling prediction under data scarcity, proposed transfer learning framework, source pretraining performance, target plant fine-tuning gains, mechanistically interpretable SHAP/LOFO shifts, and industrial MBR cross-scenario validation.\n- Section 1: Introduction: Operational challenges of MBR fouling, limitations of data-driven modeling in data-limited plants due to sparse EPS/SMP monitoring, promise of transfer learning, explainability requirements, and study objectives.\n- Section 2: Materials and methods: Data collection across target and source pilot MBRs (2.1); physicochemical analytical assays including Lowry, Dubois, LC-OCD, and EEM (2.2); data preprocessing and feature selection (2.3); pretraining base models on source plants (2.4); baseline target model training (2.5); transfer learning fine-tuning workflows (2.6); performance evaluation metrics (2.7); and cross-scenario testing on an industrial MBR (2.8).\n- Section 3: Results and discussion: Source–target feature distribution and PCA overlap analysis (3.1); construction and performance of base LSTM and XGBoost models supported by SHAP and source biomass characterization (3.2); transfer learning performance across fine-tuning ratios, model comparison, LOFO/SHAP attribution shifts, target-plant physicochemical verification of Fe-protein fouling pathways, and industrial MBR validation (3.3).\n- Section 4: Implications and outlook: Practical implications for data-limited wastewater facilities, surrogate optical fouling fingerprints (UV-vis, EEM), full-scale hydrodynamics, and inorganic/organic coupling.\n- Section 5: Conclusions: Summary of transferable EPS-centered fouling mechanisms, efficacy of LSTM-FT with limited calibration data, scientific interpretability of plant-adaptive feature shifts, and deployment outlook."
